\begin{abstract}
Generative models of discrete {3D} structure --- LEGO assemblies, voxel
buildings, block worlds --- are evaluated almost exclusively by borrowing the
image pipeline: render the object from a few views, embed the renders with a
pretrained encoder, and report a Fr\'echet or kernel distance against real data.
We show that this pipeline is structurally blind to whether a structure has an
inside.

Our probe, \solidify{}, fills every \emph{sealed} void of a held-out corpus of
real buildings with that build's own dominant material: a median $33\%$ more
blocks, $94\%$ of builds altered, the exterior silhouette untouched. A
multi-view DINOv2 kernel distance places the result $\mathbf{0.38}$ noise floors
from real --- under half its own sampling noise --- while a kernel distance on
D4-invariant voxel descriptors places it $\mathbf{202}$ away.

The failure belongs to the representation, not to one statistic or one encoder.
On a second domain (LEGO bricks) \textbf{all seven} render-space statistics we
test --- KID, MMD, Fr\'echet, precision, recall, density, coverage --- fail to
resolve the probe while resolving every other corruption we apply. No encoder
(CLIP, ImageNet ResNet-50, the Inception network FID itself uses) and no view
budget (up to $16$ views, covering roof and underside) clears two noise floors;
sixteen views separate it \emph{less} well than four. The same encoder and the
same statistic separate it at $9$--$57$ noise floors the moment the renders show
the inside. The interior is absent from the input, not mishandled by the
features --- and the metric is blind in both directions, scoring a hollowed
corpus $0.4$ floors from real against $156$ for the geometric metric.

We build the evaluation that sees it. The geometric metric is blind in the
complementary direction --- a material permutation moves it by \emph{exactly}
zero --- so we combine the two by worst pillar rather than mean, with
memorization and mode collapse as disqualification gates, since a realism-only
ranking is won by reciting the training set. Every metric must first pass
blocking ordering, invariance and sample-size gates, which Fr\'echet distance,
coverage, density and precision all fail. We also make explicit a known but
unexploited compression: squared-MMD metrics respond quadratically to the
fraction of an arm that is bad (measured exponent $1.99$), so an arm failing a
quarter of its prompts reads $6.4\%$ of the way to fully bad; the square root
recovers the failure rate to a mean absolute error of $0.011$ against $0.124$.

The suite's credibility rests on a case where it cost us a result. A provenance
audit revealed $87\%$ test contamination in our best-scoring generator;
retraining on a clean split moved it out of a significance group of its own and
into the group containing the baselines it had appeared to beat. Auditing this
paper turned up a second, smaller contamination in those baselines --- which is
the point: without a gate that makes every arm declare its training split, this
is invisible. We release the suite, probes, baselines and split.
\end{abstract}
